Intelligent materials that can undergo physical gelation in response to environmental stimuli have potential impacts in the bioengineering and biomedical fields where the entrapment of cellular or molecular species is desired. Here, we utilize atomic force microscopy (AFM) to perform molecular level investigations of designer artificial proteins that undergo physical gelation. These are engineered as triblock copolymers with independent interchain binding and solvent retention functions, namely, two terminal leucine zipper-like peptide sequences and a central alanylglycine rich sequence, respectively. AFM force measurements between probes and surfaces functionalized with molecules of this triblock protein revealed adhesive interactions that increased in average force and frequency as the pH was lowered from $\mathrm{pH}11.2$ to 7.4 to 4.5, reflecting an increase in the numbers of interacting molecular strands. In bulk solution, lowering the $\mathrm{pH}$ results in a viscous liquid to gel transition. The modular design of the triblock protein was also exploited for single molecule force spectroscopy investigations, which revealed altered intramolecular interactions in response to changes in pH. An increased understanding of the inter- and intramolecular forces involved in biomolecule driven gelation processes is not only of great fundamental interest in the study of the biomolecular systems involved but may also prove key in enabling the rational design of new generations of intelligent hydrogel systems.
## Introduction
Controlled gelation has potential impacts on the drug delivery field and other bioengineering applications requiring encapsulation or the controlled release of living cells and labile bioactive substances. For such applications, it may be desirable to avoid chemical cross-linking methods to induce hydrogel formation as these generally employ toxic crosslinking agents that may be detrimental to the entrapped moieties.1 The use of physically cross-linked gels, where reversible gelation may be induced under mild aqueous conditions in response to a stimulus such as pH, temperature, ionic strength, or specific analytes, may offer a more attractive solution.2-9 Achieving tight control over the physical gelation process remains an important research goal.
A rational approach to achieve intelligent control over physical gelation is to design molecular systems that incorporate independent interchain binding and solvent retention functions.2 In this study, we investigate the molecular level interactions between artificial multidomain proteins with such a design. Specifically, the previously described2 triblock architecture (3) of these proteins consists of relatively short leucine zipper end blocks 1, which provide an interchain binding capability, and a water-soluble polyelectrolyte domain 2 that imparts a solvent retention function and prevents precipitation of the protein (Figure 1). Each end block 1 comprises a Helix region, which can associate with another Helix region to form a dimeric coiled-coil structure10 of six heptad repeats $(a-b-c-d-e-f-g)$ in length (Figure 1a). Since 9 out of 12 of the $e$ and $g$ positions are occupied by glutamic acid residues, the coiled-coil structure is destabilized in basic solutions through glutamate to glutamate repulsive interactions across the dimer interface.11,12 Indeed, bulk solution studies have revealed that the monodisperse triblock copolymer 3 exhibits a pH-dependent gel point lying between $\mathrm{pH~}8.0$ and 9.5 as indicated by a progressive transformation from elastic to viscoelastic to viscous behavior as the $\mathrm{pH}$ is raised.2
Here, we perform molecular level investigations of the triblock copolymer 3 utilizing atomic force microscopy (AFM) force measurements and single molecule force spectroscopy (SMFS) to reveal changes in the inter- and intramolecular interactions of 3 in response to $\mathrm{pH}$ . AFM has been employed previously in the study of specific intermolecular interactions in receptor-ligand systems13-16 and has been used to reveal the intramolecular forces within individual domains of large multidomain proteins.17,18 Its application here is to help elucidate a biomolecule driven gelation process on a molecular level by revealing the fundamental inter- and intramolecular interactions involved.
## Experimental Procedures
Materials. All the proteins investigated here were prepared by bacterial expression of the corresponding artificial genes.2 Unless otherwise indicated, materials were obtained from Sigma-Aldrich Co. Ltd. (Gillingham, Dorset, UK) and were of analytical grade quality.
General Methods. All pH adjusted solutions were $10\mathrm{mM}$ $\mathrm{NaH_{2}P O_{4}}$ and $150\ \mathrm{mM\NaCl}$ that were adjusted to $\mathrm{pH}4.5$ , 7.4, and 11.2 with $1\mathrm{MHCl}$ or $1\ \mathrm{M\NaOH}$ unless specified. All solutions were prepared using high purity deionized water, purified on an ELGA water system (Maxima HPLC, Elga Ltd., Bucks, UK), with resistivity approximately $15\mathrm{{M}}$ $\Omega$ cm. Solutions were degassed and filtered using a $0.2\mu\mathrm{m}$ filter prior to use (Sartorius A. G., Go¨ ttingen, Germany).
AFM Force Measurements. Template stripped goldcoated glass substrates19 were utilized for all experiments, and AFM probes (Digital Instruments, Veeco Metrology group, CA) were gold-coated as previously described.20 The AFM probes and substrates were subsequently functionalized with proteins $_{1-3}$ , by incubation in a $\mathrm{pH}7.4$ solution of the proteins $(10\mu\mathrm{M})$ for $2{-}3\mathrm{~h~}$ . All protein-coated probes and substrates were then rinsed in a high $\mathrm{pH}$ solution to promote dissociation of formed coiled-coil moieties and to remove any noncovalently bound protein.
Force measurements were performed in liquid environments at constant piezo extension/retraction velocities of 1 $\mu\mathrm{m~s}^{-1}$ using a custom-built $\mathbf{AFM}^{21}$ or a Molecular Force Probe instrument (Asylum Research, CA). The spring constants of the cantilevers were calibrated using the thermal excitation method22 and were found to lie between 0.009 and $0.012\mathrm{N}\mathrm{m}^{-1}$ . For each experimental condition explored, measurements were typically performed until at least 100 curves were collected, and a record was also made of the percentage of measurements displaying no adhesion.
All forces and distances are expressed as mean values of the recorded adhesion data (stating the standard deviation of the data around this value). It should be noted that although there are several potential sources of error within this type of measurement, the error that likely has the most impact is that associated with the calibration of the cantilever spring constant (uncertainty estimated at $\sim10\%^{23}$ ). Throughout these experiments, however, we aimed to reduce the influence of this uncertainty through the comparison and superposition of data sets obtained first with the same cantilever and then with that obtained with different cantilevers.
## Results and Discussion
Intermolecular Interactions between Molecules of the Triblock Protein 3. Force measurements were performed between probes and surfaces functionalized with 3 to investigate their intermolecular interactions as a function of pH. Typical force traces for each pH condition are displayed in Figure 2. It should be noted that these measurements differed completely in appearance to control measurements recorded in these and previous experiments24 between goldcoated probes and gold-coated substrates. Such measurements were found to yield a mean adhesion force of $777\ \mathrm{pN}$ (SD 390, $n=51$ ) and had the appearance of typical nonspecific adhesion curves with sharp clean rupture points.25 These characteristics meant that they could easily be distinguished from the force curves resulting from specific protein-protein interactions, such as those presented in this paper.
At $\mathrm{pH}11.2$ , greater than $90\%$ of the force traces displayed no adhesion (Figure 2, curve i). At this $\mathrm{pH}$ , the mean force for curves displaying adhesion was $36.7\ \mathrm{pN}$ (SD 15.7, $n=$ 27) (Figure 2, curve ii). In a $\mathrm{pH}~7.4$ environment, $93\%$ of the force curves collected had a single adhesion point (Figure 2, curve iii), and $7\%$ had multiple adhesion points (Figure
2, curve iv). The mean force of adhesion $(48.8\mathrm{pN}$ (SD 37.7, $n=67$ ) was greater than that measured at $\mathrm{pH}11.2$ . In a pH 4.5 environment, all the force curves collected displayed adhesion, and $86\%$ of the curves displayed multiple adhesion points (see Figure 2, curve v). The magnitude of the mean recorded adhesion $(145.7\mathrm{pN}$ (SD 95.8, $n=104,$ ) was also markedly increased.
When performing biomolecular interaction force measurements, information on the likely number of molecular interactions broken during a rupture event can be estimated from the frequency that a probe-sample adhesion event is observed in a particular experiment.26-28 In other words, the increase in the frequency of the recorded adhesion forces as the $\mathrm{pH}$ is lowered is consistent with an increase in the number of intermolecular interactions occurring between molecules of 3. We can therefore postulate that in the bulk solution, these intermolecular interactions may be responsible for the formation of network junction points that would increase in number as the $\mathrm{\pH}$ is lowered.2 As stated already, a viscous liquid to gel transition is observed in bulk solutions of these proteins, as the $\mathrm{pH}$ is lowered.
Any additional impact on the aggregation of these molecules due to changes in the forces of these intermolecular interactions is difficult to assess with the current experimental setup as the exact number of interactions detected during the measurement is unknown and likely to be multiple. Indeed, when the force traces collected at $\mathrm{\pH}$ 4.5 were scaled to the same contour length (at a force of 50 pN, the relative extension was set equal to one), the force extension profiles were not all superimposable (data not shown), suggesting that during the force measurements, several molecules were stretched simultaneously. This difficulty in assigning the observed features in the force curve at the single molecule level prompted an investigation of the intramolecular forces present within single molecules of protein 3.
Intramolecular Forces within the Triblock Protein 3. Molecules of 3 were first immobilized onto gold substrates via their terminal cysteine residues and then SMFS measurements made utilizing gold-coated AFM probes. In such measurements, single molecules of protein 3 were mechanically extended as the probe and surface were separated in $\mathrm{pH}~4.5$ , 7.4, and 11.2 environments, to investigate the intramolecular interactions present under each of these conditions. During the SMFS measurement, molecules are typically attached to the probe surface through physisorption, a process of probe attachment that can lead to high attachment forces over tens of seconds without creeping.17,18,29,30 In these experiments, molecules were found to remain attached to probe surfaces up to forces of $150~\mathrm{pN}$ (SD 89.0, $n>100_{.}^{.}$ ).
Figure 3 displays a typical force extension trace for an SMFS measurement of 3 at $\mathrm{pH}~7.4$ . At this $\mathrm{pH}$ , approximately $45\%$ of the force curves displaying adhesion events contained both an adhesion point at a and mechanical stretching/unfolding of the protein at c at greater probesample separations (Figure 3). Of the remainder of the force curves displaying adhesion, a further $45\%$ displayed only the adhesion at a, and $10\%$ did not display this initial adhesion but did display the mechanical stretching of the protein at c. The subgroup of force curves displaying adhesion only at a corresponded to those measurements in which the protein became detached from the tip before further extension could occur.
The subgroup of force curves containing both the features at a and c will be discussed in further detail here due to the reproducibility of these observed features. In this subgroup of force curves, the adhesion point at a was always of a similar appearance, with a mean recorded rupture force of $29.8~\mathrm{pN}$ (SD 4.1, $n=24$ ) and a length of $22.3~\mathrm{nm}$ (SD 2.7 nm). At point b, the cantilever has returned to its resting position. Molecule(s), however, remain tethered to the probe and are mechanically stretched again at c as they approach their contour length. The nonspecific attachment to the probe is ruptured at $\mathbf{d}$ .
A wormlike chain model (WLC) (eq 1)31
$$
F(x)=\frac{k_{\mathrm{B}}T}{p}\bigg(\frac{1}{4(1-x/L)^{2}}-\frac{1}{4}+\frac{x}{L}\bigg)
$$
was utilized to model the molecular extension at c for the force curve in Figure 3 (see red fit). The persistence length $p$ describes the polymer stiffness, $k_{B}$ is Boltzmann’s constant, $L$ is the contour length, and $T$ is the temperature. The persistence length $p$ models the polypeptide elasticity and is dependent on the force range. The wormlike chain model was fitted here for forces greater than $50\mathrm{~\pN}$ , and a persistence length of $0.4\ \mathrm{nm}$ was found to describe the data well, suggesting that the molecular extension observed at c results from the forced extension of a single polypeptide chain.30,32,33 The contour length for the molecule, also derived from this fit, was found to be $88~\mathrm{nm}$ , in agreement with the calculated contour length of $87.4\mathrm{nm}$ (assuming $0.38{\mathrm{nm}}$ per residue).33
For comparison of the force-extension profiles of different lengths (due to the probe picking up molecules at various points along their length), all traces were scaled to the same contour length (at a force of $50~\mathrm{pN}$ , the relative extension was set equal to one). Seven of the representative scaled force curves are shown in Figure 4, where the extension portion of their curves can now be seen to superimpose (with a resulting change in the apparent shape of the adhesion at the beginning of the curve), confirming that single molecule force extensions of 3 are being recorded.29,33 This is in marked contrast to the force curves recorded between probes and substrates both functionalized with 3, where the force extension traces could not be superimposed in such a manner when scaled.
Because of the modular nature of 3, it was possible to perform concurrent independent AFM investigations of both proteins 2 and 1 to allow molecular level fingerprinting of the regions in the force curve in Figure 3. SMFS measurements of protein 2, a random coil polypeptide, displayed a molecular extension similar to c in Figure 3 without an initial adhesion point similar to a (data not shown). A persistence length of $0.4\mathrm{nm}$ and a contour length of $34\mathrm{nm}$ were derived by the WLC model in eq 1 being fitted for forces greater than $50\mathrm{pN}$ , reflecting the decrease in the calculated contour length of the molecule relative to 3 (34 vs $87~\mathrm{nm}$ ).
Values of $25{-}35\mathrm{pN}$ have previously been reported when the triple helical coiled-coils found in spectrin34 were unfolded by SMFS.30 In a separate study, we have also recently investigated the intermolecular forces between the individual helices of a coiled-coil, which has a similar thermal unfolding transition to spectrin at near physiological $\mathrm{pH}$ ( $T_{\mathrm{m}}$ for spectrin3 ${}^{4}\sim53~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $T_{\mathrm{m}}$ for protein ${\bf1}^{2}\sim50$ $^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ ).24 At $\mathrm{pH}7.4$ , the peak force in the force distribution for protein 1-protein 1 interactions was found to be approximately $30\mathrm{pN}$ , and the observed adhesion events were found to stretch between 20 and $25~\mathrm{{nm}}$ prior to the rupture event.24 Thus, the initial peak a observed in Figure 3, comparable in both rupture length and force to the one stated previously, may be indicative of the unfolding/rupture of an individual coiled-coil complex, formed from two interacting protein 1 regions (Figure 5). Importantly, such adhesion events were never observed during the SMFS measurements of protein 2, further suggesting that the adhesion point a in Figure 3 is attributable to the presence of the leucine zipper Helix domains in 3. Once the Helix regions are mechanically separated and the hydrophobic core of the coiled-coil is exposed, solvent destabilizing effects on the individual $\mathfrak{a}$ -helices cause the peptide chains to adopt a random coil conformation.35 This would lead to the relaxation observed in the force-extension trace at b until the entire sequence of 3 is unfolded at c (Figure 5). The observation of a small subgroup of measurements displaying only c is also consistent with this model, and they most likely correspond to proteins in which an intramolecular coiled-coil complex has either not formed (possibly due to influence of the surface)
or that has spontaneously dissociated at a force beneath the noise floor of the measurement prior to extension of the molecule.
Our previous circular dichroism studies of the protein in bulk solution have revealed that the helicity of the Acys coiled-coil increases as the pH is decreased, indicating a greater propensity for coiled-coil formation at low pH.36 There are many similar reports in the literature of the destabilizing effect of glutamate to glutamate interchain $i$ to $i^{\prime}+5$ repulsion at high pH.11,12,36,37 Conversely, at low $\mathrm{pH}$ , the glutamate to glutamate interaction has been found to be stabilizing as the presence of protonated glutamic acid residues act like nonpolar residues by extending their side chain methylene groups across the hydrophobic interface and strengthening the hydrophobic core while also reducing solvent accessibility.38,39 It follows that the propensity for the Helix regions of 3 to be engaged in a coiled-coil suprastructure will decrease with an increase in $\mathrm{\pH}$ as observed in the intermolecular force measurements between molecules of 3 (Figure 2). This is also reflected in the SMFS measurements of 3 performed at $\mathrm{pH}11.2$ , where $90\%$ of the recorded force curves do not display the adhesion point at a and only display the stretching of the unfolded polypeptide chain at c. Conversely, the force extension traces from SMFS measurements of 3 at $\mathrm{pH}~4.5$ always display an adhesion point at a of significantly larger magnitude (ca. $118\mathrm{pN}$ ) than that recorded at $\mathrm{pH}~7.4$ or 11.2. At this lower $\mathrm{pH}$ , the formation of higher-order coiled-coil aggregrates (i.e., through interactions with neighboring molecules on the sample surface) cannot be excluded.
## Conclusion
Achieving tight control over physical gelation processes may have significant impact in bioengineering and biomedical fields where the entrapment of cellular or molecular species is desired. Here, we have performed molecular level AFM investigations of artificial proteins with a triblock architecture that undergo physical gelation in bulk solution. From these studies, it is apparent that the number of both inter- and intramolecular interactions between and within the proteins varies dramatically with pH. Force measurements recorded between probes and surfaces functionalized with molecules of the artificial protein revealed adhesive interactions that increased in average force and frequency as the $\mathrm{\pH}$ was lowered. We postulate that these attractive intermolecular forces may lead to the formation of network junction points in the bulk solution that could in part account for a viscous liquid to gel transition observed in the bulk solution as the $\mathrm{\pH}$ is lowered. A fingerprinting of the intramolecular interactions involved was, in addition, gained through SMFS measurements of the proteins and also supports this model. The increased understanding of such fundamental biomolecular inter- and intramolecular forces could ultimately enable tighter control over similar physical gelation processes by facilitating the rational design of novel intelligent hydrogel systems.
